// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

// The world this crate declares itself against.
//
// # Why it exports a function
//
// **Because an import nobody calls does not survive the linker.** The first version of this world had the
// import and no export, and the built component came out as
//
//     package root:component;
//     world root {
//     }
//
// — **empty.** The `extern` block `wit_bindgen::generate!` emits is dead code until something references
// it, so the artifact said nothing about whether the import exists. **A build that cannot show the
// import is a build that cannot be told apart from one where the binding was never generated**, which is
// the shape of every instrument defect this project has recorded.
//
// So `run-assertions` exists to *use* the macros. It is not an API anyone should call in anger; it is
// the reference that keeps the import alive, and `wasm-tools component wit` on the artifact is what
// proves the macros expanded into calls on `qqq:test/assertions` rather than into nothing.
//
// # Why it imports and does not export `qqq:test/assertions`
//
// Because the host implements it. A guest that asserted against its own implementation would be checking
// itself, and the whole point of the audit stream being the host's is that the guest cannot see what it
// was given — only, through this interface, what it did.
//
// # Why this file declares its own package
//
// Because a world file is not exempt from the rule that every WIT file names a package. Without this
// line `wit_bindgen::generate!` fails with *"no `package` header was found in any WIT file for this
// package"*, and the failure it produces next — `cannot find module or crate qqq` — is a cascade from
// the macro not expanding at all, which is worth knowing so the second error is not mistaken for the
// first.
//
// **And the name must differ from the package it imports.** `qqq:test@1.0.0` is the interface below; a
// world that took that name would be a second declaration of it. `examples/orders-api` does the same:
// `package qqq:app@1.0.0` imports `qqq:http@1.0.0`.
//
// # Why the interface is vendored at `deps/qqq-test/qqq-test.wit`
//
// WIT reads a **directory** of `.wit` files as one package, so a guest's own world and the packages it
// imports cannot share a directory — the same rule `host_http.rs` records from the host side, where
// pointing `bindgen!` at `wit/` fails with *"package identifier `qqq:ai@1.0.0` does not match previous
// package name of `qqq:agent@1.0.0`"*. The host reads the file from `wit/qqq-test.wit`; a guest gets a
// copy under `deps/`, which is what `examples/orders-api/wit/deps/qqq-http/` does for `qqq:http`.
//
// **A copy is a second answer to "what is `qqq:test`", and the two can drift.** That risk is not
// hypothetical, and `tools/check_wit_vendoring.py` exists because it happened: `wit/qqq-http.wit` was
// corrected to admit that `send` has no transport, and the vendored copies were left asserting the
// retracted guarantee — *"a false security property, in the file a guest author's toolchain actually
// consumes."* The checker enforces byte-equality between every `deps/` copy and `wit/`, and it reads
// this file: with it in place this copy is *"correct exactly once"* rather than silently stale.
package qqq:test-guest@1.0.0;

world qqq-test {
  /// The assertions a component under test makes about itself — `TEST-007`, `TEST-008`.
  import qqq:test/assertions@1.0.0;

  /// Exercise both macros, so the import above is referenced and survives into the component.
  ///
  /// # Why this is an export rather than a `#[cfg(test)]` function
  ///
  /// Because a test function is not reachable from a WIT world, and **only something the world can reach
  /// keeps the import in the artifact.** The same reason `examples/orders-api` exports its handler: a
  /// component's contract is what a host can call.
  export run-assertions: func();

  /// Assert a capability name that does not exist, so the misspelling path is reachable.
  ///
  /// # Why a reference app exports something that fails
  ///
  /// Because this is not an app — it is the crate the macros live in, and `run-assertions` above is
  /// already documented as *"a reference, not an API."* **The failing paths need a caller that can reach
  /// them**, and only an export keeps an import in the artefact.
  ///
  /// `assert_caps!("clock.wal")` — one letter short. The host has no such capability, so
  /// `assert-caps-only` answers **`unknown-capability`** rather than a verdict, and the macro panics with
  /// the name. That is the WIT's own reason: *"a typo in a capability name would otherwise assert nothing,
  /// and pass. **This is the one error here that is almost always a test bug.**"*
  export assert-caps-unknown: func();

  /// Mark, then assert a limit that cannot be met, so the *recording* path is reachable.
  ///
  /// # Why the limit is zero
  ///
  /// `assert-fuel-below` records a failure when `consumed < limit` is false — and for `u64` that
  /// comparison is false for every consumption when `limit` is `0`. **So this export fails without needing
  /// the guest to burn a specific amount of fuel**, which would make the reference depend on the engine's
  /// cost model rather than on the macro.
  ///
  /// **And it distinguishes the two ways an assertion can go wrong**: with the mark below, the failure is
  /// *recorded* and the call succeeds; without it the host answers `no-fuel-baseline` and the macro
  /// panics. The mark is here, so this is the recording path.
  export assert-fuel-exceeded: func();
}
